00:13:38.052 --> 00:13:39.486
from its different names

00:13:39.486 --> 00:13:42.220
it feels like there are tons of projects running simultaneously

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:44.053
the feeling is like there's something happening

00:13:44.053 --> 00:13:45.420
Google brand

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.087
And then I also have DeepMind

00:13:48.087 --> 00:13:49.454
When do you think this was

00:13:49.454 --> 00:13:51.221
Starting to gradually converge

00:13:51.221 --> 00:13:52.421
And up until now

00:13:52.421 --> 00:13:54.355
This relatively better situation

00:13:54.355 --> 00:13:56.488
I think this matter actually

00:13:56.488 --> 00:13:58.522
May also have some connection with the company culture

00:13:58.522 --> 00:13:59.289
Indeed

00:13:59.289 --> 00:14:01.656
Google, being a software company

00:14:01.656 --> 00:14:04.223
So software companies tend

00:14:04.223 --> 00:14:05.490
To be more like that

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:09.190
a diverse approach to management where multiple methods flourish together

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.224
because it might think that

00:14:11.224 --> 00:14:12.824
software is the kind of thing

00:14:12.824 --> 00:14:14.224
that can suddenly emerge and take off

00:14:14.224 --> 00:14:16.691
A new idea

00:14:16.691 --> 00:14:18.792
It might have come from the person on the left side

00:14:18.792 --> 00:14:20.225
Or it could be from the person on the right side

00:14:20.225 --> 00:14:21.759
Or maybe from someone behind you

00:14:21.759 --> 00:14:24.392
It could also be the person before you doing it

00:14:24.392 --> 00:14:25.859
So when working at Google

00:14:25.859 --> 00:14:27.193
You often get this feeling

00:14:27.193 --> 00:14:29.093
It's like when I'm working on this thing

00:14:29.093 --> 00:14:31.227
But there are also five teams

00:14:31.227 --> 00:14:34.861
Five other groups are working on this

00:14:34.861 --> 00:14:40.228
So software companies' organizational structure

00:14:40.228 --> 00:14:45.729
Tends to favor this bottom-up approach

00:14:45.729 --> 00:14:48.196
Hardware companies tend to be more top-down

00:14:48.196 --> 00:14:50.196
One of the reasons for this is that

00:14:50.196 --> 00:14:52.330
hardware is essentially

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:54.930
a concept of integration

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:58.131
You want everything to fit perfectly

00:14:58.131 --> 00:15:00.198
So it will be more top-down

00:15:00.198 --> 00:15:03.965
Not to mention hardware companies like Apple

00:15:03.965 --> 00:15:09.199
Where the phone case and its screen..

00:15:09.199 --> 00:15:11.032
How to integrate them together

00:15:11.032 --> 00:15:14.233
It's all extremely precise

00:15:14.233 --> 00:15:17.800
So this kind of company's culture

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:19.600
Will be more top-down

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.201
Because it has the capacity to spare

00:15:21.201 --> 00:15:24.201
It can simultaneously develop various projects

00:15:24.201 --> 00:15:25.201
And the question you asked

00:15:25.201 --> 00:15:27.702
What makes it particularly good is one reason

00:15:27.702 --> 00:15:31.035
Because software is currently undergoing a major transformation

00:15:31.035 --> 00:15:33.202
Because the previous approach to software

00:15:33.202 --> 00:15:36.203
typically involved developing many different modules

00:15:36.203 --> 00:15:37.670
and completing them all

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:39.003
and then integrate it

00:15:39.003 --> 00:15:41.204
integrate them together

00:15:41.204 --> 00:15:45.204
but now with the emergence of large models

00:15:45.204 --> 00:15:48.205
many things are done using the converge method

00:15:48.205 --> 00:15:51.205
It's a module that handles many different tasks

00:15:51.205 --> 00:15:56.206
So this direction of software development

00:15:56.206 --> 00:16:01.740
Is becoming more like a top-down organization

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.207
Speaking of this point

00:16:03.207 --> 00:16:06.207
I have to say because of this

00:16:06.207 --> 00:16:08.474
that is Sergey Brin

00:16:08.474 --> 00:16:11.208
the co-founder

00:16:11.208 --> 00:16:12.208
greatly

00:16:12.208 --> 00:16:13.575
He did make a comeback, didn't he?

00:16:13.575 --> 00:16:17.209
At some point, it seemed like he really got serious and came back

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:22.210
Do you think his comeback also helped make things more unified?

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:23.210
Yes, definitely

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:29.211
Moreover, his personality is very proactive

00:16:29.211 --> 00:16:31.211
He's the type of person who really enjoys getting things done

00:16:31.211 --> 00:16:35.845
He's a very execution-focused type of founder

00:16:35.845 --> 00:16:37.645
Because I saw in the interview he was just wearing shorts

00:16:37.645 --> 00:16:38.912
then at Google

00:16:38.912 --> 00:16:40.912
very relaxed

00:16:40.912 --> 00:16:42.079
very chill

00:16:42.079 --> 00:16:43.079
super chill

00:16:43.079 --> 00:16:47.613
But he's actually very proactive in what he does

00:16:47.613 --> 00:16:50.747
He may appear very chill on the surface

00:16:50.747 --> 00:16:52.547
But in reality he's extremely proactive

00:16:52.547 --> 00:16:54.214
Because I saw in his interview that

00:16:54.214 --> 00:16:57.148
He probably stepped down around 2019 or 2020

00:16:57.148 --> 00:16:59.015
Meaning he stopped being CEO

00:16:59.015 --> 00:17:01.182
Then it was somewhat like being forced into retirement

00:17:01.182 --> 00:17:04.216
Then one day he happened to meet someone from OpenAI who said

00:17:04.216 --> 00:17:09.216
Why are you in this retired state? Now is the time to fight the most.

00:17:09.216 --> 00:17:10.017
You should come back.

00:17:10.017 --> 00:17:11.217
He seemed to have been provoked later.

00:17:11.217 --> 00:17:16.584
And then he came back—now we have to return to working in the office

00:17:16.584 --> 00:17:20.785
Yes, and in fact he has always been the President of Technology

00:17:20.785 --> 00:17:22.585
during his time at Google

00:17:22.585 --> 00:17:25.219
As for the CEO, that was Larry Page

00:17:25.219 --> 00:17:29.819
As for Sergey, he has always been more focused on..

00:17:29.819 --> 00:17:32.453
the direction of technology R&amp;D

00:17:32.453 --> 00:17:35.220
but to some extent you could think of it this way

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.687
that is to say, Google went through a period

00:17:37.687 --> 00:17:40.421
in fact, you could call it peacetime

00:17:40.421 --> 00:17:41.621
Yes

00:17:41.621 --> 00:17:42.855
15 years later

00:17:42.855 --> 00:17:46.222
As we just discussed, this topic is a great example

00:17:46.222 --> 00:17:49.756
He might think that this 0.1% improvement

00:17:49.756 --> 00:17:51.523
500 million dollars

00:17:51.523 --> 00:17:53.223
For him, it's not exciting

00:17:53.223 --> 00:17:55.957
Although for us developers, we might think

00:17:55.957 --> 00:17:57.490
that's interesting

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:00.791
If this idea could be realized, there would be a

00:18:00.791 --> 00:18:05.191
for example, a 0.5% or even 1% improvement

00:18:05.191 --> 00:18:07.792
so at Google Research

00:18:07.792 --> 00:18:11.926
we were constantly looking for those 5% or 10% improvements

00:18:11.926 --> 00:18:15.226
or even higher levels of disruption

00:18:15.226 --> 00:18:17.226
We actually managed to find it in the end

00:18:17.226 --> 00:18:20.027
And the disruption turned out to be even greater than we imagined

00:18:20.027 --> 00:18:23.794
Especially since the transformer concept was originally

00:18:23.794 --> 00:18:27.028
proposed by researchers at Google at that time

00:18:27.028 --> 00:18:29.795
The result is a flourishing of diverse ideas

00:18:29.795 --> 00:18:33.662
Ultimately, the credit still goes back to Google

00:18:33.662 --> 00:18:36.196
Yes, and you could say that

00:18:36.196 --> 00:18:40.563
Such an important discovery is actually very difficult to..

00:18:40.563 --> 00:18:44.897
Tied up inside a box

00:18:44.897 --> 00:18:47.031
This thing will naturally

00:18:47.031 --> 00:18:48.698
Because its power is too great

00:18:48.698 --> 00:18:52.398
Its capability is too remarkable

00:18:52.398 --> 00:18:55.399
So this thing is really about

00:18:55.399 --> 00:18:57.832
I personally believe it's truly changing the world

00:18:57.832 --> 00:18:59.833
How could you possibly

00:18:59.833 --> 00:19:01.166
let it go

00:19:01.166 --> 00:19:02.900
No way

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:07.967
It really started after the .com boom in 2000

00:19:07.967 --> 00:19:10.234
I think even he himself said this should be

00:19:10.234 --> 00:19:15.001
Considered the greatest leap in human development

00:19:15.001 --> 00:19:17.802
I originally thought that maybe

00:19:17.802 --> 00:19:19.202
the development of mobile phones

00:19:19.202 --> 00:19:23.403
smartphones has already advanced tremendously

00:19:23.403 --> 00:19:25.036
so I originally thought that

00:19:25.036 --> 00:19:27.703
to keep up with the internet

00:19:27.703 --> 00:19:30.204
or the development of smartphones

00:19:30.204 --> 00:19:32.537
any new invention during my lifetime

00:19:32.537 --> 00:19:34.604
might not necessarily be something I'll get to see

00:19:34.604 --> 00:19:39.205
Really? And then these large models emerged

00:19:39.205 --> 00:19:42.205
I think it's quite possible

00:19:42.205 --> 00:19:44.206
And the more I look at it, the more likely it seems

00:19:44.206 --> 00:19:47.206
Right now it feels highly probable

00:19:47.206 --> 00:19:50.673
Yes, about two or three years ago, people might have still been saying...

00:19:50.673 --> 00:19:52.474
Just when ChatGPT first came out

00:19:52.474 --> 00:19:55.207
It felt like there was a real, sudden pause in the conversation

00:19:55.207 --> 00:19:58.208
And at that time, we were transitioning from Lambda to Bard

00:19:58.208 --> 00:19:59.675
this journey

00:19:59.675 --> 00:20:01.908
and finally evolved into the Gemini journey

00:20:01.908 --> 00:20:04.409
is also because

00:20:04.409 --> 00:20:07.209
there are many different people within Google

00:20:07.209 --> 00:20:10.209
I feel that when we were working on Lambda

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:13.210
and Lambda's predecessor was a project called Mina

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:15.077
when we were working on Mina and Lambda

00:20:15.077 --> 00:20:16.610
many people thought that

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:19.644
This feels like a toy

00:20:19.644 --> 00:20:21.211
Same as GPT2

00:20:21.211 --> 00:20:23.411
At that time, I think many people looked at GPT2

00:20:23.411 --> 00:20:24.878
It seemed like it could do something

00:20:24.878 --> 00:20:26.579
It's like you can't really do anything

00:20:26.579 --> 00:20:27.645
That doesn't seem to work either

00:20:27.645 --> 00:20:28.912
Yes

00:20:28.912 --> 00:20:32.246
So do you really think that later

00:20:32.246 --> 00:20:35.213
It's amazing compared to when it was still like a toy

00:20:35.213 --> 00:20:39.214
What are the main differences in using it

00:20:39.214 --> 00:20:42.081
I think the feeling at that time

00:20:42.081 --> 00:20:45.381
There's a bit of similarity to a book in the business field

00:20:45.381 --> 00:20:47.415
It's particularly interesting when discussing this

00:20:47.415 --> 00:20:49.215
The Innovator's Dilemma

00:20:49.215 --> 00:20:50.615
It's highly relevant

00:20:50.615 --> 00:20:56.216
The feeling when using Lambda was that

00:20:56.216 --> 00:20:59.217
it's fun to chat with it

00:20:59.217 --> 00:21:05.218
But does this actually have any real use?

00:21:05.218 --> 00:21:08.051
And its accuracy might not be that high

00:21:08.051 --> 00:21:10.685
Sometimes it says some really strange things

00:21:10.685 --> 00:21:13.452
Plus there's the hallucination problem

00:21:13.452 --> 00:21:16.219
People might think these hallucination issues

00:21:16.219 --> 00:21:18.719
might not be fixable

00:21:18.719 --> 00:21:20.220
can't be fixed

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.887
then let's wait and see

00:21:22.887 --> 00:21:24.420
So I think in fact

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:28.221
we started from sequence

00:21:28.221 --> 00:21:31.221
this sequence transduction

00:21:31.221 --> 00:21:33.988
in 2015, before Transformer

00:21:33.988 --> 00:21:36.855
Another paper I consider even more important

00:21:36.855 --> 00:21:40.056
That paper on sequence to sequence learning

00:21:40.056 --> 00:21:42.490
At the time, we really should have realized

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:45.690
This was a transformative technology

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:48.824
but I think many people missed it

00:21:48.824 --> 00:21:50.524
missed it in 2015

00:21:50.524 --> 00:21:52.524
the Transformer paper came out in 2017

00:21:52.524 --> 00:21:53.691
and missed it again

00:21:53.691 --> 00:21:57.192
By 2019 or 2020

00:21:57.192 --> 00:22:03.226
When we were discussing whether to launch this Lambda

00:22:03.226 --> 00:22:04.693
Everyone missed it too

00:22:04.693 --> 00:22:08.227
We had to wait until OpenAI released ChatGPT

00:22:08.227 --> 00:22:12.361
And the main people inside ChatGPT

00:22:12.361 --> 00:22:16.195
Several of them were from my former team

00:22:16.195 --> 00:22:18.195
So you can see that

00:22:18.195 --> 00:22:20.228
Some people on the team think

00:22:20.228 --> 00:22:22.229
this is transformer technology

00:22:22.229 --> 00:22:25.229
while others still consider it just a toy

00:22:25.229 --> 00:22:28.230
The Innovator's Dilemma means that

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:30.930
often a new technology

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:34.197
may initially appear to be just an imitation

00:22:34.197 --> 00:22:36.731
or even a counterfeit product

00:22:36.731 --> 00:22:37.898
very cheap

00:22:37.898 --> 00:22:39.231
yeah exactly

00:22:39.231 --> 00:22:42.232
It doesn't feel like Google search, right

00:22:42.232 --> 00:22:45.199
but we still put it up there

00:22:45.199 --> 00:22:46.199
We're Google, you know

00:22:46.199 --> 00:22:48.199
Yeah, so it's like

00:22:48.199 --> 00:22:52.200
Just like we were talking about Xerox earlier

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.567
At that time, Xerox had laser printing

00:22:55.567 --> 00:22:57.201
Then later came inkjet

00:22:57.201 --> 00:23:00.134
When inkjet first came out, Xerox thought

00:23:00.134 --> 00:23:01.935
This thing is too terrible

00:23:01.935 --> 00:23:04.202
The print quality was very poor

00:23:04.202 --> 00:23:06.202
low resolution

00:23:06.202 --> 00:23:08.302
One of these is actually quite interesting to talk about

00:23:08.302 --> 00:23:10.202
because of laser printing

00:23:10.202 --> 00:23:12.203
it uses toner particles

00:23:12.203 --> 00:23:14.503
Use this heat laser

00:23:14.503 --> 00:23:18.404
Attach it to this paper

00:23:18.404 --> 00:23:21.471
Inject it as a liquid

00:23:21.471 --> 00:23:23.438
Spray it by injecting

00:23:23.438 --> 00:23:25.205
Inkjet printer

00:23:25.205 --> 00:23:26.872
We call it an inkjet printer

00:23:26.872 --> 00:23:29.205
It sprays the ink onto the paper

00:23:29.205 --> 00:23:32.206
"Spraying ink" sounds kinda lame

00:23:32.206 --> 00:23:33.539
Is that what it means?

00:23:33.539 --> 00:23:35.773
So the effect of spraying it in

00:23:35.773 --> 00:23:37.206
Its resolution in fact

00:23:37.206 --> 00:23:40.073
Was much lower than laser printing at that time

00:23:40.073 --> 00:23:42.707
So the printed results were extremely poor

00:23:42.707 --> 00:23:44.207
So the people at Xerox thought

00:23:44.207 --> 00:23:47.208
This is an inferior technology

00:23:47.208 --> 00:23:49.408
Why should we invest in this thing

00:23:49.408 --> 00:23:52.409
So when we were working on Mina lambda

00:23:52.409 --> 00:23:55.676
many people shared the same feeling

00:23:55.676 --> 00:23:57.209
they thought this thing

00:23:57.209 --> 00:23:59.743
could never be replaced

00:23:59.743 --> 00:24:02.177
existing technology

00:24:02.177 --> 00:24:04.410
So this is the Innovator's Dilemma

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:06.644
Yes, because actually

00:24:06.644 --> 00:24:08.578
Given today's atmosphere at that time

00:24:08.578 --> 00:24:10.311
I also think Google is really in a dilemma

00:24:10.311 --> 00:24:12.578
Because it's like saying

00:24:12.578 --> 00:24:13.812
Now I'm having hallucinations

00:24:13.812 --> 00:24:16.712
And after the hallucinations are output

00:24:16.712 --> 00:24:18.946
I must say even with today's AI

00:24:18.946 --> 00:24:20.513
there's still an overview

00:24:20.513 --> 00:24:21.580
there actually still is

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.580
but now people don't see it as a problem

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.647
It's now at an acceptable level

00:24:25.647 --> 00:24:27.214
because everyone knows

00:24:27.214 --> 00:24:30.381
that all AIs include everything, meaning

00:24:30.381 --> 00:24:32.681
OpenAI, Claude, or any other company

00:24:32.681 --> 00:24:33.548
Everyone has hallucinations

00:24:33.548 --> 00:24:36.215
So now Google having hallucinations seems

00:24:36.215 --> 00:24:38.282
not particularly serious

00:24:38.282 --> 00:24:39.749
People would think

00:24:39.749 --> 00:24:41.682
It's only been three years

00:24:41.682 --> 00:24:43.716
People no longer see this as a serious issue

00:24:43.716 --> 00:24:45.016
Quite remarkable

00:24:45.016 --> 00:24:50.317
Yes, and I think this shows how quickly technology acceptance evolves

00:24:50.317 --> 00:24:56.085
Or even users' evolution and expectations of them

00:24:56.085 --> 00:24:57.218
will keep changing

00:24:57.218 --> 00:25:00.218
Take our mobile phones for example

00:25:00.218 --> 00:25:02.219
Back in the day when we saw those big brick phones

00:25:02.219 --> 00:25:04.219
There's a mobile phone in the car

00:25:04.219 --> 00:25:05.219
What happened then?

00:25:05.219 --> 00:25:08.220
Wow, amazing! So impressive!

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:09.887
And what was it called before?

00:25:09.887 --> 00:25:12.220
What is BB call called in Taiwan?

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:14.721
1997 BB call

00:25:16.021 --> 00:25:19.221
Now who among us has a mobile phone

00:25:19.221 --> 00:25:22.122
It has become completely commonplace

00:25:22.122 --> 00:25:23.922
It's a very common thing, even with smartphones

00:25:23.922 --> 00:25:26.856
You'd only find it strange if you didn't have one

00:25:26.856 --> 00:25:29.856
So now we're at the point where

00:25:29.856 --> 00:25:34.057
These have already become integrated into our daily lives

00:25:34.057 --> 00:25:36.924
So you start to take it for granted

00:25:36.924 --> 00:25:39.224
So I think this kind of AI technology

00:25:39.224 --> 00:25:43.692
Is currently going through this same kind of transition

00:25:43.692 --> 00:25:48.359
Because Google I/O was just one or two weeks ago

00:25:48.359 --> 00:25:50.192
What do you think is inside?

00:25:50.192 --> 00:25:52.193
Although that event was truly

00:25:52.193 --> 00:25:53.326
I think it's

00:25:53.326 --> 00:25:55.227
Personally, I believe it's been the past decade

00:25:55.227 --> 00:25:57.760
Google's best Google I/O

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:58.994
Although many people disagree

00:25:58.994 --> 00:26:00.227
But that's how I feel

00:26:00.227 --> 00:26:04.861
I'm the only one in this editorial department who uses Android

00:26:04.861 --> 00:26:08.195
And every time it's always during Apple's keynote

00:26:08.195 --> 00:26:10.195
And everyone gets super excited waiting for it

00:26:10.195 --> 00:26:15.229
But when it's Google's turn, nobody pays attention to me

00:26:15.229 --> 00:26:17.430
Thank you for being our Google fan

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.597
I told you this feature is awesome

00:26:19.597 --> 00:26:21.230
But then no one was listening to me

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.197
Oh, really?

00:26:24.197 --> 00:26:28.131
But it's also because there was just too much content this time

00:26:28.131 --> 00:26:31.065
Is there one that you personally feel

00:26:31.065 --> 00:26:33.132
whether it's the company or you personally

00:26:33.132 --> 00:26:34.932
you feel is your favorite

00:26:34.932 --> 00:26:38.399
I would say now, last year 2024

00:26:38.399 --> 00:26:40.933
When we announced at I/O

00:26:40.933 --> 00:26:42.933
I said "Oh my God"

00:26:42.933 --> 00:26:45.367
Now this multi-modality capability

00:26:45.367 --> 00:26:48.768
The so-called multi-modality refers to this large model

00:26:48.768 --> 00:26:51.701
Have you noticed multimodal

00:26:51.701 --> 00:26:54.469
Have you noticed we used to often talk about

00:26:54.469 --> 00:26:57.669
large language models, which are LLMs, right

